  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCirrus clouds from Subtropical Storm Josephine early this \nmorning will quickly give way to sunny skies as Josephine\nexits further out to sea. Northerly winds become breezy by mid \nto late morning given plenty of sunshine and a tight pressure \ngradient inbetween exiting Josephine and an approaching \nshortwave from Canada. Expect northerly winds to become \nsustained 5-12kts with gusts up to 20-25kts (strongest along and\neast of the Hudson River) as sufficient boundary layer mixing\nallows stronger winds aloft to mix to the sfc. Clouds increase \na bit late this afternoon into early evening as the trough axis \nand moisture-starved cold front push through with northerly \nwinds remaining breezy (gusts up to 15-20kts) into this evening.\nWinds gradually weaken after Midnight but remain elevated given\nthe lingering pressure gradient. Subsidence and sfc high \npressure building south/east out of Canada will allow sunny \nskies to return on Friday with cool and notable dry air \nadvection (PWATs likely fall under 0.25\") resulting in \nseasonably cool daytime temperatures only rising into the 50s. \nIdeal radiational cooling Friday night will likely result in \nwidespread temperatures falling near or under 30 degrees by \nearly Saturday morning. High pressure remains in control through\nthe weekend before sliding southward early next week. This \nwill likely allow zonal flow to ensue, advecting a milder air \nmass into the Northeast for Sunday and especially Monday. Our \ndry stretch likely persists through at least Wednesday as the \nlatest NBM probabilistic guidance shows near or less than a 10% \nchance for a tenth of an inch of rain.",
